# ReturnDesk

- Follow `docs/PRD.md` as the source of truth. If an installed skill conflicts with the PRD, the PRD wins.
- Stack: Next.js App Router, TypeScript, Tailwind, PostgreSQL, Drizzle, Zod. Route handlers run on the Node.js runtime.
- Enforce every business rule on the server and with database constraints. The UI may hide illegal actions; it is not the enforcement layer.
- Never replace persistent PostgreSQL data with in-memory arrays, JSON files, or browser storage.
- Keep route handlers thin. Put validation, business rules, data access, and error mapping in shared server modules.
- Use transactions and row locks when an operation depends on current status. Keep those transactions short.
- Money is PostgreSQL `NUMERIC`, never a float. Request references come from a sequence, never from counting rows.
- Run relevant tests after each phase. Do not claim a feature is complete without command output.
- Stay inside the current phase. Do not refactor unrelated code or add out-of-scope features (auth, email, payments, inventory).
- Do not commit secrets. `.env` stays untracked; `.env.example` lists every variable.
- Stop and report if an acceptance criterion cannot be met.
- Use installed skills only when the task matches them.
